import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap

## The posthog package: an app's errors sent to PostHog's error tracking, from the app itself (a
## middleware, two route handlers and a plugin's loop), grouped by fingerprints of its own; and the daily digest
## that reads them. Static: PostHog is a server.
meta = {
	title: "PostHog",
	description: "packages/posthog, a plugin for Roc Solid's server: 5xx answers, errors the app deals with, jobs that failed for good and errors in the browser, sent to PostHog's error tracking, grouped into issues, with a daily digest that reads them.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "what", label: "What it reports" },
		{ id: "capture", label: "An error the app handles" },
		{ id: "where", label: "Where they go" },
		{ id: "issues", label: "Issues" },
		{ id: "digest", label: "A daily digest" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not reported" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/posthog</code>]}
				title="Errors in PostHog, grouped into issues, from the app itself"
			>
				5xx answers, errors the app handles but wants seen, jobs that failed for good, and uncaught errors in
				a browser, sent to <a href="https://posthog.com/docs/error-tracking">PostHog's error tracking</a> with
				where they happened and who they reached.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				The package is a <a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugin</a>, a
				<a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a> and two route handlers, written in Roc on what
				any app can do (the platform has no error-reporting hook). The middleware notices failed answers,
				your routes take the browser's errors with the package's handlers, and a background loop sends
				everything to PostHog in batches, so a request never waits on PostHog.
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in the app's header (<code>{"posthog: \"…/packages/posthog/main.roc\""}</code>), then
				wire it into the program in four places:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.posthog_program.file}, in part"} source={Sources.posthog_program.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={Sources.posthog_middleware.file} source={Sources.posthog_middleware.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The middleware</strong>, <code>PostHog.middleware(user)</code>, goes in
					<code>routes/+middleware.roc</code>, after the middleware that knows who is asking. <code>user</code>
					maps that middleware's context to the person's id, or <code>""</code> when nobody is known. With no
					such middleware, put PostHog's first, with <code>{"|_| \"\""}</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The plugin</strong>, <code>{"PostHog.plugin({})"}</code>, goes in the app's plugins; it reads
					its key from the environment (<a href="#where">where they go</a>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The routes</strong> for the browser's errors are yours:
					<code>routes/rocx-errors/+server.roc</code> answers with
					<code>{"PostHog.errors_route!(request)"}</code>, and <code>routes/rocx-errors.js/+server.roc</code>
					with <code>{"PostHog.script_route(request)"}</code>, the page's script, which sends to its own path
					without <code>.js</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Failed jobs</strong> are reported when the <a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>jobs</a> plugin is
					given <code>{"on_failed!: PostHog.job_failed!"}</code>.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				For the browser's errors, add your route's script to the layout:
				<code>{"<script src=\"/rocx-errors.js\" defer></script>"}</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="what">What it reports</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>What</th><th>How</th><th>Type in PostHog</th><th>Handled</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>an answer with a 5xx status</td><td><code>PostHog.middleware(user)</code></td><td><code>ServerError</code></td><td>no</td></tr>
						<tr><td>an error the app dealt with</td><td><code>PostHog.capture!(message, fields)</code></td><td><code>Error</code></td><td>yes</td></tr>
						<tr><td>a job that failed for good</td><td><code>on_failed!: PostHog.job_failed!</code></td><td><code>JobFailed</code></td><td>no</td></tr>
						<tr><td>an error or a rejected promise nothing caught in a browser</td><td><code>/rocx-errors.js</code></td><td>the error's own (<code>TypeError</code>)</td><td>no</td></tr>
						<tr><td>a page that wasn't what the app renders, so the runtime rendered <code>{"<body>"}</code> again (<a href={"${Paths.platform_browser}#hydration"}>a hydration mismatch</a>)</td><td><code>/rocx-errors.js</code></td><td><code>HydrationMismatch</code></td><td>no</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A 5xx report carries the route's pattern (<code>/posts/[id]</code>, not the path with its ids), the
				method, the status, the first line of the answer's text (unless it is HTML), and who asked. An error
				from nobody known creates no person in PostHog.
			</p>
			<p>
				Errors wait in the server's memory (<a href={Paths.platform_kv}>Kv</a>, the newest 1,000). The loop
				sends them every ten seconds, and once more as the server stops, as <code>$exception</code> events to
				PostHog's batch endpoint.
			</p>

			<Heading id="capture">An error the app handles</Heading>
			<p>
				Most of what goes wrong in a Roc app is a value, not a crash: a refusal, a database error, a fetch that
				timed out. To report one the app handles, call <code>PostHog.capture!</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.posthog_capture.file}, in part"} source={Sources.posthog_capture.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>capture!</code> takes a message and a record of fields, as <code>Log.error!</code> does. Called
				while answering a request, it adds that request's path and method.
				<code>{"PostHog.capture_for!(user, message, fields)"}</code> also names the person it reached, so
				PostHog can count the people an issue affects.
			</p>

			<Heading id="where">Where they go</Heading>
			<p>
				Give each option to <code>{"PostHog.plugin({ … })"}</code>, or set the first three in the environment;
				code wins.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Environment</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>key</code></td><td><code>POSTHOG_KEY</code></td><td>none: reporting is off</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>host</code></td><td><code>POSTHOG_HOST</code></td><td><code>https://us.i.posthog.com</code> (use <code>https://eu.i.posthog.com</code> for EU Cloud)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>release</code></td><td><code>POSTHOG_RELEASE</code></td><td>none; set it to the commit a build is from</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>interval_ms</code></td><td></td><td><code>10000</code> (at least 100)</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Without a key nothing is kept or sent, as you want while developing. At start, the server logs where
				errors go, or that reporting is off.
			</p>

			<Heading id="issues">Issues</Heading>
			<p>
				PostHog groups errors into issues, usually by their stack trace. Roc has no stack traces, so the package
				tells PostHog which issue each error belongs to (its <code>$exception_fingerprint</code>):
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>a 5xx answer, by its route's pattern and its message;</li>
				<li>a captured error, by its message alone, so it is one issue wherever it happens;</li>
				<li>a failed job, by its kind and its error;</li>
				<li>a browser's error, by its type and message.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Parts that change between occurrences are taken out first: any word with a digit counts as the same
				(<code>user 1234 not found</code> and <code>user 77 not found</code> are one issue), and so does text
				in double quotes or backticks.
			</p>
			<p>
				So a loop of errors can't flood the project, one issue is sent at most 20 times a minute, all
				browsers together send at most 120 errors a minute, and each page load at most 10. A batch PostHog
				doesn't take (a 5xx, or no answer) is sent again next time; one it refuses (a 4xx, such as a wrong
				key) is dropped, and the log says so.
			</p>

			<Heading id="digest">A daily digest</Heading>
			<p>
				PostHog's MCP server lists the issues and how many people each reached. A scheduled agent can read it
				once a day, write a digest of what is new or came back, and for each top issue check out its
				<code>release</code>, write a failing test from its route, method and fields, fix the bug on a branch
				and open a pull request for a person to review.
				The <a href="https://github.com/Roc-Solid/rocx/tree/master/packages/posthog">package's README</a> has
				the steps.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not reported</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A crash in Roc while answering a request.</strong> It ends the call before the middleware sees
					an answer; the host answers 500 and logs <code>rocx server: Roc crashed</code>. (A job that crashes is
					reported: to the jobs plugin, a crash is a failure.)
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Some failures in the browser.</strong> A computation that fails with no
					<code>{"<Errored>"}</code> around it, and a handler that fails, are written to the browser's console by
					the runtime and never reach the script.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
